the employees at Heckert have  experienced a lot in the past 25 years  under communism 4300 people worked in  what was then a state-owned  machine-tools factory but german  reunification in 1990 bought rapid  change a new beginning privatisation  bankruptcy and another new beginning  over the years thousands of employees  lost their jobs staff constantly feared  for their livelihood Heckert is now  owned by a swiss investor and business  is looking up and les Tilda has seen it  all the design engineer has worked it  for 30 years and has been a member of  the company's works counselor for  fifteen of them I think it's always  important that the company is healthy  because that's the only way we can hold  on to our jobs and that's ultimately  what counts Tilda is on first-name terms  with sales manager Michele Fiedler he  sees himself as a mediator between  management and the workforce Heckert has  dropped out of Germany's collective  labor agreements that means that unions  no longer negotiate wages instead  employee representatives negotiate  directly with their bosses today nearly  half of companies in Germany work this  way  it's easier for us to work this way than  to accept contracts agreed somewhere  else that perhaps wouldn't suit us if we  can't communicate directly with each  other and solve the problems then why  should others be able to do it no  collective wage agreement that usually  means less take-home pay and that rule  of thumb also applies at heckard but  they trust their works council chairman  to get the best deal for them about 90%  of employees voted for tilt nur in the  last election here we go states based on  market conditions have been some lean  years when he held off on demands our  main competitors are no longer based in  Italy or Spain today they come mainly  from Asia China Japan Taiwan or Korea  that means we can't worry about German  export surpluses or a lack of purchasing  power when it comes to the level we're  competing at I think we've struck a very  good balance between economic  achievement and letting the employees  benefit there are no temporary workers  or freelance work contracts here just  permanent staff the employees appreciate  this feeling of security it also makes  the company attractive to workers from  the region  most often people ask me about  interesting tasks what the work climate  is here how teams work together in such  questions it's amazing that money isn't  always the first thing on their minds of  course the employees would still welcome  more money early next year Tilda will  begin a new round of wage negotiations  but first he'll take a close look at the  company's finances to see how much more  money he can extract for his colleagues  the majority would spend it and if they  saved it it would only be because  they're uncertain about the future  uncertainty is exactly what and last  owner wants to avoid he believes there's  no benefit to higher wages if they lead  to job cuts later
 

